{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When you acquire a Hebrew slave.</b> Perhaps it intends to say that if both a Canaanite slave and a Hebrew slave are available for purchase before him, he should give precedence to the Hebrew one. And this is the verse's measure: when you wish to acquire a slave — give precedence to acquiring a Hebrew one, and do not say, I will acquire a slave whom I may work forever, and I will not acquire a slave whom after six years I must send free." "And the reason it called him <b>Hebrew</b> is that the verse was sensitive to attributing the name of servitude to Israel; therefore it euphemized him with this name. It further alluded that the name of servitude upon Israel is transitory and not permanent, for they are servants to HaShem, and he goes free from his hand in the seventh year. It further hints that he did not come to this condition until he had transgressed against the Torah and commandments — and this is in accord with their words, of blessed memory (Kiddushin 14b), for the verse speaks of one whom the court sold for his theft; and also one who sells himself due to his hardship, as we find that the verse says (Deuteronomy 15:12) ״כִּי יִמָּכֵר לְךָ אָחִיךָ הָעִבְרִי״ — he too did not come to this until transgressions had already preceded, as per the statement of Rabbi Ami (Shabbat 55a): there are no afflictions without sin." "There is a further fine point to examine in its saying <b>when you acquire a Hebrew slave</b>, and not saying 'a Hebrew, a slave' — to hint that before you acquire him he is already a slave, as they said, of blessed memory (Kiddushin 22b), for the verse speaks of one whom the court sells, and he had already incurred obligation from Heaven. And with this there is no difficulty as to why it was necessary to say the word 'slave': for from this we might have mistakenly inferred a slave of a Hebrew (Mekhilta Mishpatim 1), so that it became necessary for another verse to spell it out, as it is taught: ״כִּי יִמָּכֵר לְךָ אָחִיךָ הָעִבְרִי״ (Deuteronomy 15:12)." "<b>Further,</b> it appears that the reason it was necessary to say 'slave' is to give a reason for why it specified six years; therefore it prefaced by saying that this one whom you acquire is already a slave, in the manner that He said (Leviticus 25:55) ״כִּי לִי בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל עֲבָדִים״:" "<b>Six years he shall serve.</b> The reason it said so is to derive that in any event he must serve six, even if he fled. And its saying <b>he shall serve</b> without specifying 'he shall serve you' is intended to include that he shall serve also upon the death of the master to his son." "<b>And in the seventh, etc.</b> Meaning: and sometimes he will also serve in the seventh year, when he is sold in the middle of a sabbatical cycle, to complete the year in which he was sold, so that there will be six full years from day to day. It further intends that he will also serve in the seventh — meaning, the seventh of the [sabbatical cycle of] years, should it fall within his six years. I have also found this precise reading in the words of Rambam in his Commentary on the Mishnah (Kiddushin chapter 1, mishnah 2)."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:31:31.140384756Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}